{"parentUuid":"acf51776-617b-4e6e-828d-140d7c255a02","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_0113zq5Jmf7g381WFUF9nyGF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good question to ask, and the answer splits along the two changes (#2 vs #3) because they touch different things:\n\n## Session data: not affected by either change\n\nSession state has always lived in `.git/entire-sessions/<id>.json`, which is anchored at `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` (the **shared** .git dir). See `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/session_state.go:25-31`:\n\n```go\nfunc getSessionStateDir(ctx context.Context) (string, error) {\n    commonDir, err := GetGitCommonDir(ctx)  // shared, pre-existing\n    ...\n    return filepath.Join(commonDir, session.SessionStateDirName), nil\n}\n```\n\nSame goes for shadow branches (`entire/<commit-hash>-<worktreeHash>`, in shared git refs) and the committed metadata branch (`entire/checkpoints/v1`, also shared git refs). None of that moves.\n\n## #3 (session-matching widening) is purely additive\n\nThe widening only ADDS matches, never removes:\n\n```\nFrom linked worktree wt-foo (before): returns sessions where WorktreePath == wt-foo\nFrom linked worktree wt-foo (after):  returns sessions where WorktreePath == wt-foo\n                                      PLUS sessions where WorktreePath == main\n```\n\nSo a user who's been working in `git worktree add ../wt-foo` and has sessions with `WorktreePath = wt-foo` — those still match strict-equal from `wt-foo` after the upgrade. Nothing is hidden. The new behavior only kicks in if there *also* happens to be a main-worktree session, which the old code couldn't see.\n\n## #2 (`.entire/` anchoring) does silently relocate some on-disk state\n\nThis is where your concern is valid. The gitignored, filesystem-local pieces that the old code wrote into the linked worktree's `.entire/` now resolve to main's `.entire/`:\n\n| file | impact |\n|---|---|\n| `wt-foo/.entire/logs/` | orphaned — still on disk but new writes go to `main/.entire/logs/` |\n| `wt-foo/.entire/settings.local.json` | **silently ignored** — CLI now reads `main/.entire/settings.local.json` |\n| `wt-foo/.entire/tmp/` | orphaned, transient anyway |\n| `wt-foo/.entire/metadata/` | mostly an in-git-tree path concept (see `temporary.go:310` — those `EntireMetadataDir` references build git trees, not filesystem files). Light filesystem usage; whatever's there gets orphaned. |\n\nThe interesting one is `settings.local.json`. If someone deliberately had per-worktree local overrides (e.g., `wt-foo/.entire/settings.local.json` with `log_level: DEBUG` for noisy debugging of that branch), the upgrade silently swaps to main's file and they'll wonder why their overrides don't apply anymore. The PR doesn't migrate or warn about this.\n\n## So the practical answer\n\n- **Session continuity: safe.** Existing sessions still work from any worktree, before and after.\n- **Auxiliary state: relocated.** Logs orphaned (annoying, not destructive). `settings.local.json` overrides silently swapped (user-visible behavior change). The PR doesn't mention this transition and there's no migration step.\n\nThat's a fair thing to call out on the PR — at minimum a NOTES section saying \"if you had per-worktree `.entire/settings.local.json` overrides in linked worktrees, they will be ignored after this change; the canonical location is now the main worktree.\" Possibly a `entire doctor` check that warns when it finds an orphan `.entire/` in a linked worktree."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":569,"cache_read_input_tokens":150266,"output_tokens":2240,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":569,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2240,"cache_read_input_toke